  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Entra Workload ID">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Entra Workload ID</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft Entra Workload ID enables managed identities at the pod level in AKS.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsoft Entra Workload ID provides managed identities for AKS workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c97b53f9c2bcaf2eaf8ee6eff46b6b2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776979406</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Entra ID">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Entra ID</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft Entra ID is Microsoft's identity and access management platform that serves as a shared foundational service and global platform dependency for almost all Azure services. It provides authentication, authorization, and role-based access control (RBAC) capabilities across the Azure ecosystem, functioning as an identity provider and critical dependency for security verification.

As a global Azure service, Microsoft Entra ID is a managed service with built-in automatic redundancy across multiple regions, implementing redundancy transparently. It offers centralized account management, application access management, and enhanced identity protection. The platform supports workload identity, service principals, and integration with various Azure services including Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) and Azure Container Registry. Many Azure services support Microsoft Entra ID as an alternative to connection strings and keys for authentication, and it is used for applying RBAC through service principals in Azure DevOps service connections.

Microsoft Entra ID serves as a typical external dependency for workloads and is commonly used for user authentication and sign-in functionality. It also appears as a component in Failure Mode Analysis (FMA) examples related to user sign-in scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c97b53f9c2bcaf2eaf8ee6eff46b6b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77437de30be507969c1117574fed4849&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ff5c56a565c7a13e4cc5066c5ed0775&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bb2c831e41151a23a478ec8c59ab787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31cf91ba30eab331729c73c4002b505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc6c19c90a7ec75e7d95d807c1817fcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9406ed2aa903d9c51a1e6e1d68aee2dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f771a609846ef0b7b2580d4b2e3191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e2cc133f2276ccf7c79c3147bf59683</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-operational-procedures.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983292</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kubernetes RBAC">
  <data key="d0">Kubernetes RBAC</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kubernetes RBAC is used with Microsoft Entra ID for least privilege access control.&lt;SEP&gt;Kubernetes RBAC is used with Microsoft Entra ID for least privilege access control in AKS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c97b53f9c2bcaf2eaf8ee6eff46b6b2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776979406</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Policy">
  <data key="d0">Azure Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Policy is a governance and compliance service that forms a key part of the Azure Resource Manager control plane. It provides a framework for enforcing and auditing security and reliability baselines across Azure platform services, supplementing RBAC by restricting actions that authorized users can perform. Azure Policy enforces security and reliability configurations for all services, remediating or prohibiting deviations at configuration-time and ensuring compliance with engineering criteria.

Azure Policy serves as a mechanism for driving compliance through enforced security and reliability conventions, automated management activities, and compliance reporting across Azure services. It is used to enforce compliance for mission-critical workloads and provides centralized governance safeguards for AKS clusters, including pod security and configuration baselines. Additionally, Azure Policy can be used to disable Azure Cosmos DB key-based metadata write access through a built-in policy definition and ensures all regional resources route operational data to the correct Log Analytics workspace.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Policy is used to ensure consistent diagnostic settings across applications and enforce configurations for Azure services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db1b2af74d4d806b70803e4d1daf8a47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bc4a6aa7dad91160ab49f8c99c2ea47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a324803e46dec295e5d9abb5a43f8a29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bb2c831e41151a23a478ec8c59ab787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31cf91ba30eab331729c73c4002b505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc6c19c90a7ec75e7d95d807c1817fcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8deba810ca14d9b278b1f9d887802f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef87f84d908a2e8bef1511aac0487905</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-data-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-health-modeling.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-operational-procedures.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/health-modeling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981625</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Policy Add-On For AKS">
  <data key="d0">Azure Policy Add-On For AKS</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Azure Policy Add-on for AKS controls pod functions and enforces policy compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5</data>
