</node>
<node id="Azure Monitor">
  <data key="d0">Azure Monitor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Monitor is an Azure platform monitoring service that is enabled by default for all Azure subscriptions. It supports three types of observability data: logs, metrics, and distributed traces. Azure Monitor collects all operational data, including Application Insights data, with charges based on the volume ingested and retention duration.

Azure Monitor gathers metrics, logs, and diagnostics from various Azure resources. For AKS resources, it collects metrics, logs, and diagnostics while supporting Prometheus metrics. For Azure Cosmos DB, it gathers key metrics and diagnostic logs, including provisioned throughput. It is also used to evaluate Azure Cache for Redis health and key metrics, and provides near real-time operational insights for evaluating database usage and sizing.

Azure Monitor provides an extensive alerting framework to detect, categorize, and respond to operational signals through Action Groups. It also serves as the operational data monitoring platform that Azure Dashboards integrates with.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Monitor is used to define alerts for unexpected key, certificate, and secret usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Monitor is a tool used to detect issues in workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Monitor is a unified data platform that integrates with Azure Monitor health models for alerting and visualization.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Monitor is a monitoring service that provides health models, metrics, and application insights for Azure resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db1b2af74d4d806b70803e4d1daf8a47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058624a53f7dcacddad189523984d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65d6d67b4e65531ad950d1206265e623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09511819f8c1ba5a16e71983efd9f8b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a26205fb34c4e32a46218f4e0a88ca82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7255c274585b0f1f482e338bbc6c26aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aee778ccfa3886c52f485cafeef0009b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bc4a6aa7dad91160ab49f8c99c2ea47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8deba810ca14d9b278b1f9d887802f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d847dca2d14a4e01f2ac8a1fb0f3723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58687539bce004e3d6c298bc58bb0198</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-data-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-health-modeling.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/health-modeling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981629</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure CNI">
  <data key="d0">Azure CNI</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure CNI is a network plugin enabling Azure or Calico network policies for traffic control within AKS clusters.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CNI is a network plugin for AKS network integration that supports a richer feature set.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Container Networking Interface networking integrates AKS clusters within virtual networks, assigning IP addresses from the same virtual network to nodes, pods, and services.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CNI is a robust and scalable network plugin recommended for most AKS scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CNI is a network plugin for AKS network integration that supports a richer feature set.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Container Networking Interface networking integrates AKS clusters within virtual networks, assigning IP addresses from the same virtual network to nodes, pods, and services.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CNI is a robust and scalable network plugin recommended for most AKS scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5321cfe4fbc08d735ac5eac02e84e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136188a7c5b35c34dacbc95825b4191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-defd991d185ed0d546fc2887007b9160</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983279</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure App Service">
  <data key="d0">Azure App Service</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure App Service is an Azure PaaS product that provides a low-friction container platform for hosting web and API workloads, serving as a feasible alternative to AKS. It operates as a multitenant environment where each instance has a limited number of outbound ports available.

Azure App Service offers built-in features such as secondary deployment slots that can be swapped after deployment, Health Checks for scale-out operations, and disaster recovery and backup capabilities. It supports zone-redundant deployments for high availability.

In practical applications, Azure App Service is used to host e-commerce websites, as demonstrated in the FMA example where it runs e-commerce website instances deployed in a zone-redundant configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure App Service is a service that can be configured to run multiple instances, such as 10 instances.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure service that supports zone-redundant deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure App Service is a multitenant environment where each instance has a limited number of outbound ports available.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c97b53f9c2bcaf2eaf8ee6eff46b6b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c437470f34ed638659632da76be560f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09511819f8c1ba5a16e71983efd9f8b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136188a7c5b35c34dacbc95825b4191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f771a609846ef0b7b2580d4b2e3191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c83649acd71df5d7f58cf6c4f1629c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-deployment-testing.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-health-modeling.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983285</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
