</node>
<node id="Azure Key Vault Managed HSM">
  <data key="d0">Azure Key Vault Managed HSM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A dedicated managed HSM offering for scenarios requiring FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8deba810ca14d9b278b1f9d887802f2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980104</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Integrity Protection">
  <data key="d0">Data Integrity Protection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data integrity protection involves encryption and key management to safeguard data without compromising application reliability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8deba810ca14d9b278b1f9d887802f2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980105</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Policy-Driven Governance">
  <data key="d0">Policy-Driven Governance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Policy-driven governance uses Azure Policy to enforce security and reliability conventions across mission-critical application services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8deba810ca14d9b278b1f9d887802f2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980105</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Machines">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Machines</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Machines are IaaS resources that require specific security considerations including hardening, update management, and access control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980105</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deploy If Not Exist">
  <data key="d0">Deploy If Not Exist</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Deploy If Not Exist (DINE) is an Azure Policy execution mode that can take several minutes to occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980105</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mission Critical Reference Implementation">
  <data key="d0">Mission Critical Reference Implementation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mission Critical reference implementation contains a wide array of security and reliability centric policies to define and enforce a sample common engineering criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980106</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application Gateways">
  <data key="d0">Application Gateways</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Application Gateways operate at Level 7 to filter and restrict egress traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980106</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Engineering Criteria">
  <data key="d0">Common Engineering Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common engineering criteria captures secure and reliable configuration definitions for all utilized Azure services, mapped to Azure Policy assignments to enforce compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980106</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Criticality">
  <data key="d0">Criticality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Criticality refers to determining the importance of each component to design appropriate reliability levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fault Isolation">
  <data key="d0">Fault Isolation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fault isolation is a strategy to prevent noncritical component failures from cascading to other components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blast Radius">
  <data key="d0">Blast Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blast radius refers to the scope and intensity of impact when a fault occurs in the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The scope of widespread effect that a failure can have across a workload's flows and components.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast radius refers to the scope of impact when a compromise or error occurs, increased by co-location and density.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast radius refers to the scope of impact from a malfunction, which reliability seeks to limit and which increased density can expand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94148f63b4b1385b51dfefa8ba2f3472&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b284d3b10b64577458ddc2798074113b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/performance-efficiency/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981328</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Preservation">
  <data key="d0">Self-Preservation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-preservation capabilities use design patterns and modularization to prevent problems from affecting downstream components.&lt;SEP&gt;Self-preservation refers to built-in measures that help a workload remain functional during incidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scale Out">
  <data key="d0">Scale Out</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling out critical components to handle variable capacity spikes and fluctuations across supported regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Redundancy">
  <data key="d0">Redundancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Redundancy in layers and application tiers minimizes single points of failure through active-active or active-passive deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;Redundancy is a fundamental strategy of deploying multiple instances of components to ensure continued operation if one instance fails.&lt;SEP&gt;Redundancy is the implementation of multiple identical instances of a workload component to contain problems and ensure system reliability.&lt;SEP&gt;Redundancy is a reliability concept involving deploying multiple components across regions to prevent single points of failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overprovisioning">
  <data key="d0">Overprovisioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Overprovisioning immediately mitigates individual failure of redundant instances and buffers against runaway resource consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Overprovisioning is a risk when teams allocate too much supply relative to demand, often caused by misjudged capacity planning or incident response scaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Overprovisioning is deploying more instances of a resource so that some remain available while others are upgraded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b284d3b10b64577458ddc2798074113b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba037f30d06665208af1b780fac7d88b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/performance-efficiency/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design For Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Design For Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design for recovery ensures workloads can anticipate and recover from failures with minimal disruption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recovery Plans">
  <data key="d0">Recovery Plans</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Structured, tested, and documented recovery plans aligned with negotiated recovery targets for quick recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Healing">
  <data key="d0">Self-Healing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Automated self-healing capabilities reduce risks from external factors and shorten the break-fix cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Self-healing refers to measures that enable a workload to automatically recover from incidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Immutable Ephemeral Units">
  <data key="d0">Immutable Ephemeral Units</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Stateless components replaced with immutable ephemeral units that provide repeatability and consistency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design For Operations">
  <data key="d0">Design For Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design for operations shifts left to anticipate failure conditions through testing and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Observable Systems">
  <data key="d0">Observable Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Observable systems correlate telemetry for monitoring, diagnostics, and holistic health status views.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Site Reliability Engineers">
  <data key="d0">Site Reliability Engineers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Site reliability engineers prioritize remediation efforts based on observability data and respond to actionable alerts.&lt;SEP&gt;Site reliability engineers are professionals responsible for mitigating live site incidents and proactively addressing potential failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980897</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backups">
  <data key="d0">Backups</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Backups are essential for restoring the system to a working state using a trusted recovery point such as the last-known good state, ensuring immutable and transactionally consistent data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980897</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Root Cause Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Root Cause Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Root cause analysis requires shared visibility across teams of dependency status and ongoing failures for effective incident management.&lt;SEP&gt;Root cause analysis is a method for identifying deficiencies in processes or implementations related to performance issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Root cause analysis (RCA) is performed when failures occur, enabled by correlated telemetry from distributed tracing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd18a4f6bd04d93d17db01be0273b02f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef87f84d908a2e8bef1511aac0487905</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/performance-efficiency/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/health-modeling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981626</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disaster Preparedness">
  <data key="d0">Disaster Preparedness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Disaster preparedness approaches in architecture design and workload operations are needed even for highly resilient systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8047163c921812913ce249dbb081c0d2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980897</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automation">
  <data key="d0">Automation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Automation minimizes human error and brings consistency to testing, deployment, and operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Automation replaces repetitive manual tasks with software that completes them quicker, with greater consistency and accuracy, reducing risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Automation is a critical dependency of workloads that should be treated as integral, supporting at-scale operations and reducing repetitive tasks and technical debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea3d1c60d4f024bf104694fc558f794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981370</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simplicity">
  <data key="d0">Simplicity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Simplicity in architecture design reduces surface area for control, minimizing inefficiencies and potential misconfigurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Predictive Alerts">
  <data key="d0">Predictive Alerts</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Predictive alerts identify potential failures before they become live incidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failure Simulation">
  <data key="d0">Failure Simulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Failure simulation involves running tests in production and pre-production environments to set realistic recovery expectations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reliability Checklist">
  <data key="d0">Reliability Checklist</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A checklist document used to drive actions after setting a reliability strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;The reliability checklist is a next-step resource referenced for ensuring reliability practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Routine Operations">
  <data key="d0">Routine Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routine operations include application revisions, security and compliance audits, component upgrades, and backup processes that impact system stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incident Learning">
  <data key="d0">Incident Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Incident learning involves continuously learning from production incidents to drive improvements based on real-life failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standards">
  <data key="d0">Standards</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Standards in code implementation, deployment, and processes provide consistency, minimize human errors, and support troubleshooting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be641287d1772cdb4176dc9a5df85b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reliability Design Principles">
  <data key="d0">Reliability Design Principles</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A set of guiding principles for building reliable workloads that can survive outages and malfunctions while continuing to provide intended functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability design principles are documented guidelines that inform workload design decisions for achieving reliability goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resilience">
  <data key="d0">Resilience</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ability of a workload to detect and withstand faults while continuing to operate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recoverability">
  <data key="d0">Recoverability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ability to restore a workload within agreed recovery targets when disruption exceeds resiliency measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Availability">
  <data key="d0">Availability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ensuring users can access the workload during the promised time period at the promised quality level.&lt;SEP&gt;Availability is a security principle focused on preventing or minimizing system downtime and degradation during security incidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design For Business Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Design For Business Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principle focused on aligning workload design with clear, documented business goals and stakeholder promises.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design For Resilience">
  <data key="d0">Design For Resilience</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principle focused on ensuring workloads continue to operate with full or reduced functionality through fault tolerance and graceful degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Graceful Degradation">
  <data key="d0">Graceful Degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A design approach where a system continues operating with reduced functionality when components fail.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Path">
  <data key="d0">Critical Path</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The set of components essential to workload operation that must be distinguished from those that can function in a degraded state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980899</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fault Tolerance">
  <data key="d0">Fault Tolerance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ability of a system to continue operating properly in the event of failure of some of its components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980900</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RTO">
  <data key="d0">RTO</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Recovery Time Objective, a target for how quickly a workload must be restored after disruption.&lt;SEP&gt;Recovery Time Objective (RTO) defines the maximum acceptable downtime before services must be restored.&lt;SEP&gt;Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is a target metric defining the maximum acceptable time for recovery procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Recovery Time Objective, the target time for restoring a solution after an outage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c83649acd71df5d7f58cf6c4f1629c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RPO">
  <data key="d0">RPO</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Recovery Point Objective, a target for acceptable data loss measured in time during a disruption.&lt;SEP&gt;Recovery Point Objective (RPO) defines the maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.&lt;SEP&gt;Recovery Point Objective, the acceptable amount of data loss measured in time, controlled by backup frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c83649acd71df5d7f58cf6c4f1629c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SLO">
  <data key="d0">SLO</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Service Level Objective, a measurable target for reliability outcomes that should be achievable and clearly communicated.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Level Objectives are critical health signals that define the desired level of service along with quality attributes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c67df0f5abb1d6d875958c4732f3ab63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef87f84d908a2e8bef1511aac0487905</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/health-modeling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981624</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design Review Checklist For Reliability">
  <data key="d0">Design Review Checklist For Reliability</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A checklist presenting recommendations for evaluating reliability, resiliency, and failure recovery strategies in architecture design.&lt;SEP&gt;The Design Review Checklist for Reliability provides recommendations for evaluating workload architecture reliability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:01">
  <data key="d0">RE:01</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Focus workload design on simplicity and efficiency, avoiding unnecessary complexity while meeting business goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:02">
  <data key="d0">RE:02</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Identify and rate user and system flows using a criticality scale based on business requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:03">
  <data key="d0">RE:03</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Use failure mode analysis (FMA) to identify potential failures, dependencies, and failure points, and develop mitigation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;RE:03 is a specific recommendation in the Azure Well-Architected Framework Reliability checklist that advises using failure mode analysis to identify potential failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:04">
  <data key="d0">RE:04</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Define reliability and recovery targets for the workload to inform design and health model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:05">
  <data key="d0">RE:05</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Add redundancy at different levels, especially for critical flows, including redundant infrastructure and multiple instances.&lt;SEP&gt;RE:05 is a reliability checklist recommendation advising to add redundancy at different levels for critical flows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981097</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:06">
  <data key="d0">RE:06</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Implement a timely and reliable scaling strategy at application, data, and infrastructure levels based on usage patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:07">
  <data key="d0">RE:07</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strengthen workload resiliency by implementing self-preservation and self-healing measures using cloud patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:08">
  <data key="d0">RE:08</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Test for resiliency and availability scenarios by applying chaos engineering principles with malfunction and load testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:09">
  <data key="d0">RE:09</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Implement structured, tested, and documented disaster recovery plans aligned with recovery targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RE:10">
  <data key="d0">RE:10</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Measure and model solution health signals by continuously capturing uptime and reliability data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaos Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Chaos Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaos engineering is a discipline of testing resiliency through active malfunction and simulated load testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reliability Tradeoffs">
  <data key="d0">Reliability Tradeoffs</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A guide exploring concepts related to tradeoffs in reliability design decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A document covering tradeoffs related to the reliability pillar of architecture design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c68f04b50833c59213a11d31e461972</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/checklist.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981434</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Replication">
  <data key="d0">Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Replication is a key reliability strategy that duplicates components, data, or geographic presence to handle concurrent node failures, but increases surface area and costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zero Trust Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Zero Trust Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Zero Trust Architecture is a security framework whose three principles can be eroded by using overly broad security rules to mitigate reliability-related configuration complexity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incident Response Plan">
  <data key="d0">Incident Response Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An incident response plan enables rapid detection and recovery and is vital for maintaining reliable workload operations.&lt;SEP&gt;An incident response plan is a well-documented plan defining standard operating procedures for preparedness, detection, containment, mitigation, and post-incident activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Replication">
  <data key="d0">Data Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data replication is a reliability pattern that copies data to survive replica malfunction, introducing additional latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Balancing">
  <data key="d0">Resource Balancing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource balancing distributes or redistributes load to healthy replicas, affecting performance of requests being balanced.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automatic Scaling">
  <data key="d0">Automatic Scaling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Automatic scaling adjusts resources in response to demand but cannot instantaneously handle sudden spikes, necessitating over-provisioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Call Rotation">
  <data key="d0">On-Call Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">On-call rotation is a rapid response process requiring technical team members to be available, incurring personnel and opportunity costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Over-Provisioning">
  <data key="d0">Over-Provisioning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Over-provisioning is a reliability tactic of using larger or more instances than currently needed to absorb demand bursts, countering performance efficiency goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Support Contracts">
  <data key="d0">Support Contracts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Support contracts with technology providers are a key component of reliable workloads, but can incur waste when the level of support is over-provisioned.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Passive Cold Standby">
  <data key="d0">Active-Passive Cold Standby</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-passive cold standby is a recovery strategy where the secondary environment is not running until needed.&lt;SEP&gt;A DR deployment pattern where the secondary region has minimal infrastructure until failover, requiring full deployment during a disaster.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Passive Warm Standby">
  <data key="d0">Active-Passive Warm Standby</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-passive warm standby is a recovery strategy with minimal pre-deployed resources for rapid failover.&lt;SEP&gt;A DR deployment pattern where the secondary region has pre-deployed infrastructure at reduced capacity for faster failover.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronous Replication">
  <data key="d0">Synchronous Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data replication where changes are written to multiple locations simultaneously, ensuring zero data loss but potentially higher latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronous replication is a data replication approach where data is written and committed to multiple locations, requiring acknowledgment from each location.&lt;SEP&gt;A data replication approach where data is written and committed to multiple locations simultaneously.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronous replication distributes data copies across multiple availability zones, ensuring all writes complete across zones before a transaction is considered done.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f88755bbf9fb155be427dfc8a8708cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba037f30d06665208af1b780fac7d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67203c92bdb0aa325b8ab2d546d9ef56</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Asynchronous Replication">
  <data key="d0">Asynchronous Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data replication where changes are written to the primary location first then copied to secondary locations, allowing some data loss but lower latency.&lt;SEP&gt;A data replication approach where changes are replicated to another location at a later time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f88755bbf9fb155be427dfc8a8708cc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Business Criticality">
  <data key="d0">Business Criticality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classification of workloads based on importance to business operations, influencing recovery priorities and investment levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="War Room">
  <data key="d0">War Room</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A centralized location or communication channel where key personnel coordinate during disaster recovery operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Drill">
  <data key="d0">DR Drill</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A planned exercise to test disaster recovery procedures and validate recovery capabilities under controlled conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failover">
  <data key="d0">Failover</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failover is the process of switching operations to a secondary environment when the primary fails.&lt;SEP&gt;Failover is the automated and/or manual shifting of production workload traffic from an unavailable region to an unaffected geographical region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failback">
  <data key="d0">Failback</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failback is the process of restoring operations to the original environment after a failover, treated as a separate process from failover.&lt;SEP&gt;Failback is the automated and/or manual shifting of production workload traffic from a failover region back to the primary region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point-In-Time Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Point-In-Time Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Point-in-time recovery is the ability to restore data to a specific moment in time, typically used to recover from data corruption or accidental changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backup">
  <data key="d0">Backup</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A backup is a copy of data stored separately from the primary system to enable data recovery if loss, corruption, or disaster occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Activation Criteria">
  <data key="d0">DR Activation Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Predefined thresholds and conditions that determine when to declare a disaster and trigger recovery procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579193d2ba0036e6c770c9b7c78ebbdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Site Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Azure Site Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Site Recovery is an Azure service used to automate failover and recovery for IaaS systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Site Recovery enables disaster recovery for Azure VMs between availability zones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed83bb2fdd642ffb5c3592b2c8243758</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981737</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Backup">
  <data key="d0">Azure Backup</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Backup is an Azure service that facilitates backup of VMs, VM-related services, and some data services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Managed Redis">
  <data key="d0">Azure Managed Redis</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Managed Redis is an Azure product with reliability and disaster recovery capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Files">
  <data key="d0">Azure Files</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Files is an Azure service with backup capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c93cee43dab578364cd13532355256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FMA Documentation">
  <data key="d0">FMA Documentation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Failure Mode Analysis documentation captures how the system behaves during disasters and how to respond.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Plan">
  <data key="d0">DR Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The DR plan is a living document that defines recovery procedures, activation criteria, and is reviewed regularly with stakeholders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tabletop Exercises">
  <data key="d0">Tabletop Exercises</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Tabletop exercises are drills where the team practices their roles, builds familiarity, and trains new members without impacting production.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Production Drills">
  <data key="d0">Production Drills</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Production-level drills are the only way to verify that a DR plan meets RTO and RPO targets in real conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776980999</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backup Strategies">
  <data key="d0">Backup Strategies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Backup strategies involve choosing tailored solutions for each service, defining retention periods, and using multi-region storage for cross-region recoverability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981000</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Failover">
  <data key="d0">Partial Failover</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Partial failover allows individual components to fail over independently, reducing complexity, cost, and RTO while maintaining partial availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9935cc9222b0d130d80a116c87875301</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981000</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Auto-Failover Group">
  <data key="d0">Auto-Failover Group</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Auto-failover group is a mechanism in Azure SQL that fails over to a secondary region during regional outages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f771a609846ef0b7b2580d4b2e3191</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981042</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mitigation">
  <data key="d0">Mitigation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mitigation strategies fall into two categories: building more resiliency and designing for degraded performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Activities identified to address problems either proactively or reactively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981043</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Degraded Performance">
  <data key="d0">Degraded Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Designing for degraded performance involves identifying failure points that disable some components while maintaining end-to-end flow functionality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981044</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failure Mode">
  <data key="d0">Failure Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of problem that can cause one or more workload components to be degraded or severely affected to the point of being unavailable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981044</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Point-Of-Failure Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Single Point-Of-Failure Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An analysis method for identifying workload dependencies that could serve as single points of failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Detection">
  <data key="d0">Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Detection refers to infrastructure, data, and app monitoring and alerting processes and procedures used to identify failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Active Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Active-Active Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-active deployment maximizes availability by running multiple instances simultaneously, each actively handling traffic with immediate failover capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc9314d7889b0915883928ca0b8ed3e9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981092</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Passive Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Active-Passive Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-passive deployment is a cost-effective DR approach where a primary instance handles traffic while secondary instances remain on standby for failover.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc9314d7889b0915883928ca0b8ed3e9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981092</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warm Spare">
  <data key="d0">Warm Spare</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Warm spare is an active-passive configuration where the secondary location runs with minimum compute and data sizing, scaled up after failover.&lt;SEP&gt;Warm spare is an active-passive design pattern that offers shorter recovery time but higher operating costs compared to cold spare.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc9314d7889b0915883928ca0b8ed3e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cold Spare">
  <data key="d0">Cold Spare</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cold spare is an active-passive configuration where secondary location compute resources are fully scaled but stopped, requiring startup before use.&lt;SEP&gt;Cold spare is an active-passive design pattern with one primary location and secondary locations where compute resources are stopped and must be started before failover, offering lower operating costs but longer recovery time than warm spare.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc9314d7889b0915883928ca0b8ed3e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure DNS Private Resolver">
  <data key="d0">Azure DNS Private Resolver</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure DNS Private Resolver supports hybrid name resolution between on-premises and cloud environments with zone redundancy and automatic self-healing.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure DNS Private Resolver is a service that supports resilient hybrid name resolution, with self-healing capabilities during zone-wide outages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DNS Failover">
  <data key="d0">DNS Failover</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">DNS failover is achieved by assigning primary and secondary DNS servers using private resolvers across different regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CanNotDelete Locks">
  <data key="d0">CanNotDelete Locks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CanNotDelete locks are Azure resource locks that prevent accidental deletion to preserve redundancy and resiliency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Storage Redundancy">
  <data key="d0">Azure Storage Redundancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Storage redundancy provides data replication options for Azure Storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Database Active Geo-Replication">
  <data key="d0">SQL Database Active Geo-Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SQL Database active geo-replication enables replication of databases across regions for resilience.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc31c34dd7feec455a5b2a2d551c3a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981094</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Expressroute">
  <data key="d0">Azure Expressroute</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure ExpressRoute is a networking service that may require manual configuration of multiple circuits for redundancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981094</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deployment Stamps Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Deployment Stamps Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Deployment Stamps pattern creates repeatable, self-contained units containing all resources required to deliver a workload, shifting from component-level to workload-level redundancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Deployment Stamps pattern is an approach for deploying workloads across multiple regions.&lt;SEP&gt;An architectural pattern used with multi-zone multi-region deployments, particularly suitable for SaaS providers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed83bb2fdd642ffb5c3592b2c8243758&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ea658bc5677a379a1105409b6be36f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981736</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Command Query Responsibility Segregation">
  <data key="d0">Command Query Responsibility Segregation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CQRS pattern separates read-write and read-only data models to add redundant read-only database instances for resilience.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981095</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database Partitioning">
  <data key="d0">Database Partitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Database partitioning improves scalability and availability by splitting data into shards so failure in one shard only disrupts a subset of transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981095</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eventual Consistency">
  <data key="d0">Eventual Consistency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Eventual consistency is a data consistency approach that reduces tight coupling and improves throughput when data is distributed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981095</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hub-And-Spoke Model">
  <data key="d0">Hub-And-Spoke Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hub-and-spoke model is a network topology pattern used to organize cloud infrastructure into logical patterns that make redundancy design easier to build and scale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polyglot Persistence">
  <data key="d0">Polyglot Persistence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyglot persistence is the concept of using different storage technologies by the same application to take advantage of each component's best capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyglot persistence is an approach that uses a combination of data store technologies to best fit different data needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="VPN Gateway">
  <data key="d0">VPN Gateway</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">VPN Gateway is a networking service whose throughput varies based on SKU, with zone redundancy support limited to certain SKUs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Flows">
  <data key="d0">Critical Flows</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Critical flows are essential workload paths that require redundancy to meet reliability targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shard">
  <data key="d0">Shard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shard is a horizontal database partitioning strategy that supports multiple storage instances with a common schema.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure OpenAI">
  <data key="d0">Azure OpenAI</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure OpenAI managed endpoints abstract underlying infrastructure complexity behind capacity units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981097</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Consistency">
  <data key="d0">Data Consistency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data consistency is the measure of how in sync or out of sync a given dataset is across multiple stores.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partitioning">
  <data key="d0">Partitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Partitioning is the process of physically dividing data into separate data stores.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Region">
  <data key="d0">Region</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A region is an Azure datacenter location used for deploying redundant resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serverless">
  <data key="d0">Serverless</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Serverless is a cloud computing model that allows adding redundancy without managing data replication or failover operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e86188fe80b60fb0b86bacfbd8970add</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Database">
  <data key="d0">SQL Database</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure SQL Database is a PaaS database service supporting automatic failover groups and active geo-replication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981099</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geo-Distant Deployments">
  <data key="d0">Geo-Distant Deployments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Geo-distant deployments spread workloads across regions hundreds or thousands of miles apart to protect against large-scale disasters, introducing higher latency and complex data consistency requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981099</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Posture">
  <data key="d0">Security Posture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security posture refers to the overall security state of a system that must be continuously improved and not allowed to degrade over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981156</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assume Breach">
  <data key="d0">Assume Breach</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assume breach is a security principle that guides defensive measures assuming an attacker may gain access, emphasizing encryption and containment.&lt;SEP&gt;Assume breach is a Zero Trust principle that designs compensating controls to limit risk and damage if primary defenses fail.&lt;SEP&gt;Assume breach is a security mindset that presupposes potential compromise and designs controls accordingly.&lt;SEP&gt;Assume breach is a security principle that assumes systems may already be compromised and plans accordingly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981156</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verify Explicitly">
  <data key="d0">Verify Explicitly</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Verify explicitly is a security principle focused on right-sizing security measures and minimizing vulnerabilities through explicit validation.&lt;SEP&gt;Verify explicitly is a Zero Trust principle ensuring only trusted identities perform intended and allowed actions from expected locations.&lt;SEP&gt;Verify explicitly is a security principle requiring verification of all access and data changes through trusted sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Verify explicitly is a security principle requiring explicit verification of all access and actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981156</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vulnerability Scanning">
  <data key="d0">Vulnerability Scanning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Vulnerability scanning detects exploits in infrastructure, dependencies, and application code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CIA Triad">
  <data key="d0">CIA Triad</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The CIA triad refers to the interrelated security principles of confidentiality, integrity, and availability.&lt;SEP&gt;The CIA triad represents confidentiality, integrity, and availability security assurances that must be preserved during and after attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Development Lifecycle">
  <data key="d0">Security Development Lifecycle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Security Development Lifecycle (SDL) is a set of best practices for maintaining security features and workload asset inventories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Asset Inventory">
  <data key="d0">Asset Inventory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A comprehensive asset inventory includes classified information about resources, locations, dependencies, and owners relevant to security.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Root-Cause Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Root-Cause Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Root-cause analysis is a post-incident activity that provides insight into the impact of breaches and drives improvements in defenses and operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Posture Management">
  <data key="d0">Security Posture Management</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Security posture management is a platform-provided feature for quantifying current security state against established baselines and setting remediation priorities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threat Intelligence">
  <data key="d0">Threat Intelligence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threat intelligence powered by security analytics enables dynamic detection of threats.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Checklist">
  <data key="d0">Security Checklist</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Security checklist is a resource for driving security actions after setting a security strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;The security checklist is a recommended next step document for implementing security practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a51316135aacca9fd878154c3dd2abcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Operation Center">
  <data key="d0">Security Operation Center</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Security Operation Center (SOC) is a centralized function within an organization that employs specialized IT professionals to detect incidents in real time and address potential threats.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Least Privilege">
  <data key="d0">Least Privilege</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Least privilege is a security principle where access is granted only on a need-to-know basis, minimizing access permissions and exposure time.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is a security principle that minimizes access based on privilege, scope, and time to limit potential damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Encryption is a method used to safeguard data at rest, in transit, and during processing, preventing unauthorized reading of sensitive data.&lt;SEP&gt;Encryption for data at rest and in transit increases resource demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c503260b531691857650e03a6b6b239</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981232</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Classification">
  <data key="d0">Data Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data classification involves categorizing data based on type, sensitivity, and potential risk, assigning confidentiality levels to guide security measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role-Based Training">
  <data key="d0">Role-Based Training</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Role-based training focuses on developing deep expertise in platform offerings and security features that address specific workload concerns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Exfiltration">
  <data key="d0">Data Exfiltration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data exfiltration refers to unauthorized data transfer resulting from malicious or inadvertent access to data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Access Controls">
  <data key="d0">Access Controls</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Access controls are mechanisms that grant access only on a need-to-know basis to protect workloads from unauthorized access.&lt;SEP&gt;Strong access controls authenticate and authorize access to systems, minimizing access based on privilege, scope, and time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Audit Trail">
  <data key="d0">Audit Trail</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An audit trail is a record of all types of access activities that supports faster detection of security incidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Audit trail maintains logs of all access activities to support faster detection, recovery, and ongoing security monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Confidentiality">
  <data key="d0">Confidentiality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Confidentiality is a security design goal focused on preventing exposure to privacy, regulatory, application, and proprietary information through access restrictions and obfuscation techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Confidentiality is a security principle focused on protecting data as it flows through system components, enforced through consistent hardening levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Compliance Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Security Compliance Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security compliance requirements are standards imposed by organizational policies, regulatory compliance, and industry standards that influence security design and assurances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SDL">
  <data key="d0">SDL</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SDL (Security Development Lifecycle) is a method that brings clarity around security features and helps maintain an inventory of workload assets and their security reports.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18570fb8b58b59977b15628b836fce56</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automation Of Repetitive Tasks">
  <data key="d0">Automation Of Repetitive Tasks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Automation of repetitive tasks is a concept that decreases the chance of human error that can create risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18570fb8b58b59977b15628b836fce56</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Integrity is a security principle aimed at preventing corruption of design, implementation, operations, and data throughout the workload lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Chain Security">
  <data key="d0">Supply Chain Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supply chain security involves continuously protecting against and detecting vulnerabilities in software dependencies during build time and runtime.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptography techniques including attestation, code signing, certificates, and encryption used to establish trust and verify data integrity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DDoS Attacks">
  <data key="d0">DDoS Attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Distributed denial of service attacks are malicious actions that cause resource exhaustion and block system access.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-In-Time Access">
  <data key="d0">Just-In-Time Access</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Just-in-time (JIT) access is a time-based security mode that replaces standing permissions to minimize risk exposure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-Enough-Access">
  <data key="d0">Just-Enough-Access</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Just-enough-access (JEA) is a security mode that limits access scope to only what is necessary for a task.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Signing">
  <data key="d0">Code Signing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Code signing is a cryptographic technique used to verify software authenticity and integrity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Data Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data integrity ensures that data remains consistent and trustworthy during incidents and after system recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attack Surface">
  <data key="d0">Attack Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Attack surface refers to the total number of exploitable vectors in a system that can be reduced through security measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981160</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Zero Trust Model">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Zero Trust Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Microsoft Zero Trust model is a security framework used as a compass to mitigate security risks in workload design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981161</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Readiness Plan">
  <data key="d0">Security Readiness Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A security readiness plan is aligned with business priorities and provides well-defined processes, adequate investments, and appropriate accountabilities for protecting assets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981161</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Segmentation">
  <data key="d0">Segmentation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Segmentation is a strategy to plan security boundaries in workload environments, processes, and team structures to isolate access and function.&lt;SEP&gt;Segmentation enables fine-grained security controls and distinct performance characteristics but can increase total ownership costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b284d3b10b64577458ddc2798074113b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/performance-efficiency/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981329</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role-Based Security Training">
  <data key="d0">Role-Based Security Training</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Role-based security training develops deep expertise in platform offerings and security features to address workload concerns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981161</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Least-Privilege Access">
  <data key="d0">Least-Privilege Access</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Least-privilege access limits permissions to the right identities, right set of permissions, right duration, and right assets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77f391cc5c3fa628d6f44144e1bef0b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776981161</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
